2 CONT Fernán Pérez de Ayala Missing in English Automatic translation Contribute Fernan Perez de Ayala ( Toledo 1305 - Vitoria 15 of October of 1385 ) was a Castilian nobleman, son of Pedro Lopez de Ayala, advance most of Murcia , and Sa
2 CONC ncha Fernández de Barroso. [ 1 ]
2 CONT
2 CONT Fernán Pérez de Ayala Lord of Ayala Personal information Birth c. 1305 ? Toledo Death October 15, 1385 Vitoria Burial Tower-chapel of the Virgen del Cabello, in Quejana Convent ( Álava ) Family Dynasty Ayala House Father Pedro Lóp
2 CONC ez de Ayala Mother Sancha Fernández Barroso Spouse Elvira Álvarez de Ceballos Offspring See offspring Shield ayala.jpg Shield of Fernán Pérez de Ayala [ edit data in Wikidata ] For other people of the same name, see Fernán Pérez (
2 CONC disambiguation) . He was Lord of Ayala , Llodio , Arrastaria , Urcabustaiz and Cuartango , major merino of Guipúzcoa in the reign of Pedro I of Castilla , major merino of Castilla in that of Enrique II , and member of the Brotherh
2 CONC ood of Arriaga . [ 2 ] He was also Lord of Torrejón de Velasco , Major advance of Murcia and Major Merino of Asturias .
2 CONT
2 CONT Index Family origins He was the best of all of his lineage and he was very afraid of God But López de Ayala He was the son of Pedro López de Ayala (1278-1331) and of Sancha Fernández Barroso, sister of Cardinal Pedro Gómez Barroso
2 CONC  de Sotomayor . His father was Adelantado mayor of Murcia, either as Juan Manuel's lieutenant or in the name of the king, until around 1331. His paternal grandparents were Sancho López de Ayala and Aldonza de Velasco and the mater
2 CONC nal Fernán Pérez Barroso and Mencía Méndez de Sotomayor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and offspring He married Elvira de Ceballos, Mrs. Escalante , a daughter of Diego Gutiérrez de Ceballos, tenant in Lerma in 1299, and one of the knights in the Cortes of 1315, who died in 1352 according to the Becerro de 
2 CONC las Behetrías , and Juana García Carrillo with whom he had the following offspring:
2 CONT
2 CONT Pedro López de Ayala . Aldonza de Ayala married to Pedro González de Mendoza . Inés de Ayala, lady of Malpica , married to Diego Gómez de Toledo . One of her daughters, Teresa de Ayala, was a lover of King Pedro I of Castilla . El
2 CONC vira de Ayala, married to Pedro Suárez de Guzmán , II lord of Batres . Mencía de Ayala, married to Beltrán Vélez de Guevara. Leonor López de Ayala, who inherited the lordship of Torrejón de Velasco and married Fernando Álvarez de 
2 CONC Toledo y Meneses , II lord of Valdecorneja , great-grandparents of García Álvarez de Toledo and Carrillo de Toledo , I duke of Alba . Diego López de Ayala , lord of Cebolla , married to Teresa de Guzmán. Juana García de Ayala, wif
2 CONC e of Juan Fernández de Padilla , bailiff of Toledo and the king's waiter. Lifetime Early years
2 CONT
2 CONT Palace-convent complex of the Casa Solar de los Ayala, in Quejana, Álava His education was entrusted to his uncle, Cardinal Pedro Gómez Barroso de Sotomayor , author of the Book of the Councils and Councilors , so he moved to Avig
2 CONC non , France in his childhood .
2 CONT
2 CONT Being the second-born son, his father sent him to court to serve as page of Leonor of Castile , future queen consort of Aragon for her marriage to Alfonso IV .
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1328, Juan Sánchez de Salcedo el Negro , lord of Salcedo and Álava, died, passing the inheritance of the lordship to lawsuits and fights for the inheritance of the Mayorazgo de Ayala among the many nephews and natural children 
2 CONC of the deceased, to whom and to avoid the abuse of the natural son, don Juan de Murga (a nearby inheritance in Ayala donated by his father in life) were warned of it by related relatives in need of support, joining him and his old
2 CONC er brother Sancho Pérez de Ayala from the Court in Toledo where they lived for two generations, claiming that his great-grandmother was the grandfather of the deceased, remaining victorious in the field after the defeat and death 
2 CONC of Juan de Murga, natural son of the deceased. Upon the death of his older brother at the hands of Juan Sánchez de Salcedo's orphan grandson, he inherited the Casa de Ayala in approximately 1332.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was knighted in Burgos in 1332 during the coronation ceremony of King Alfonso XI of Castile, who confirmed him in the dominion of Álava, as this region was unified to the crown of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT Political activities His life exemplifies the emergence of a new type of nobility, oriented to services to the monarchy; obtaining of perks: manors and income; unification and institutionalization of the lineage with the creation 
2 CONC of the mayorazgo ; search for marriage bonds with related lineages.
2 CONT
2 CONT The diplomatic genius, the stately grace, the literary hobbies of the Barrosos were marvelously united in him, with the serene and cautious courage of the Ayala Juan de Contreras and López de Ayala, Marquis of Lozoya He held vario
2 CONC us diplomatic posts, being ambassador to Aragon in 1349, and even before ambassador to France . It was found in the siege of Gibraltar in 1349. In 1352 it pacified Las Encartaciones from Biscay , by order of King Pedro I of Castil
2 CONC e , taking them from Juan Núñez de Lara . This same year in Soria he witnessed the signing of the peace treaty with Aragon.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1354 he was one of the leaders of the Castilian nobles who questioned Pedro I of Castile about his attitude towards Queen Blanca de Borbón in Tejadillo ; meeting in which a new governance structure was presented to the monarchy
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT In the civil war between Pedro I of Castile and his brother Enrique de Trastamara , he participated first in favor of the king, supporting him in 1362 in the battle of Guadix . However, he changed sides in 1366 along with his son 
2 CONC Pero , fighting and supporting Enrique in the Battle of Nájera (1367). He participated in the siege of Toledo in 1369. Enrique II rewarded him with the Greater Advancement of Murcia in 1370, and later the Greater Merindaje of Astu
2 CONC rias .
2 CONT
2 CONT Last years Starting in 1371, already 66 years old, he retired to his lordship of Ayala , which had increased in 1349 by buying the valleys of Llodio , Orozco and Oquendo from Leonor de Guzmán , together with the strong house of Ma
2 CONC rquina and the palaces of Abendaño and Burceña . He had previously received from Pedro el Cruel , the Cuartango valley . Enrique II de Castilla confirmed the possession of Llodio , Orozco and the monastery of Respaldiza.
2 CONT
2 CONT Preparing his succession, he granted jurisdiction to Ayala , founded the mayorazgo of the House of Ayala on December 12, 1373 in favor of his son Pero , founded the Dominican monastery of Quejana where he professed before February
2 CONC  12, 1378, the year in which he granted his third will , in the Dominican order, retiring there until his death in 1385 at the age of eighty.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1371 he had published his work, «Book of the Lords of Ayala Lineage, from the first named Don Vela to my Don Fernan Perez, who made him honor and glory of God, and for my lineage, so that they may be good And homiles and serve 
2 CONC God, and those who come from the King to me. Which was compiled in the year of the Nativity of MCCCLXXI, running the era of MCCCCIX years », for the greater glory of his lineage .
2 CONT
2 CONT He was buried in Quejana, in the chapel of the Virgen del Cabello, next to his wife. Before he had donated to the monastery, the reliquary of the Virgin of the Hair that he had inherited from his uncle the cardinal, a relic preser
2 CONC ved in the convent. [ 3 ]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was survived by a son, six daughters, and several grandchildren and great-grandchildren, some of whom became the ancestors of most of the royal houses of Europe.
2 CONT
