1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gonzalo_Fernandez_de_Cordova
2 CONT
2 CONT Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba, Duke of Terranova and Santangelo, Andria, Montalto and Sessa, also known as Gonzalo de Córdoba (Italian: Gonsalvo of Consalvo di Cordova; September 1, 1453 – December 2, 1515) was a Spanish general fi
2 CONC ghting in the times of the Conquest of Granada and at the Italian Wars reputed to be "the Father of Trench Warfare" and the infantry units which were later known as tercio.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born at Montilla, in what is now the province of Córdoba, a cadet son of Pedro Fernández de Córdoba, count of Aguilar, and his wife Elvira de Herrera. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He and his elder brother, Alonso, became orphans while very young boys. The counts of Aguilar carried on an hereditary feud with the rival house of Cabra, in spite of both family branches coming from a same family tree. As a cadet
2 CONC  child within the family, he could not expect much on the way of inherited wealth or titles, having, as nearly always then, to try a church or a military career, the latter being more satisfactory to his wishes.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was first attached to the household of Don Alfonso, the king's half brother, and upon his death devoted himself to Prince Alfonso utherine sister, Isabel of Castile, who later, mediating a civil war, had proclaimed herself succ
2 CONC essor queen in 1474, disputing the right of her niece, Juana, to ascend the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the ensuing civil war between the followers of the daughter of deceased King Henry IV of Castile, Juana la Beltraneja, and the king half sister Princess Isabella of Castile, there were conflicts with Portugal as the king of
2 CONC  Portugal Afonso V of Portugal sided on the war to protect his 13 years old niece, Juana, he fought under the grand master of the Order of Santiago, Alonso de Cárdenas. After the battle of Albuera, the grand master gave him specia
2 CONC l praise for his behavior.
2 CONT
2 CONT Role in the conquest of Granada
2 CONT
2 CONT During the ten-year long conquest of Granada under the Catholic Monarchs, he completed his apprenticeship under his brother Alonso, the grand master of Santiago, Alonso de Cardenas, and the counts of Aguilar and of Tendilla, of wh
2 CONC om he spoke always as his masters. It was a war of sieges and the defence of castles or towns, of skirmishes, and of ambushes in the defiles of the mountains. The skills of a military engineer and a guerilla fighter were equally e
2 CONC mployed. Córdoba's most distinguished feat was the defence of the advanced post of Íllora. Able to speak Berber Arabic, the language of the emirate, he was chosen as one of the officers to arrange the capitulation, and, with the p
2 CONC eace of 1492, was rewarded with a grant of land in the town of Loja, near the city of Granada.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, as a widower, one of the ladies in waiting to Queen Isabella I of Castilla, namely, Luisa Manrique de Lara, on 14 February 1489, when he was already aged 36 . His surviving only daughter, Elvira Fernández de Córdoba y 
2 CONC Manrique, would inherit all their titles on his death in 1515. In order to keep, hopefully, her father name, she married within the close family, just with someone from a long time antagonistic branch but bearing also her own fami
2 CONC ly name, "Fernández de Córdoba".
2 CONT
2 CONT In Italy
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1494, King Frederick IV of Naples took power as the second inheriting son of Royal bastard king Ferdinand I of Naples. When the Catholic Monarchs decided to support the Aragonese house of Naples against Charles VIII of France, 
2 CONC Gonzalo, in his mid forties, was chosen by the influence of the queen, and in preference to older men, to command the Spanish expedition. In Italy, he won the title of the Great Captain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Italian historian Francesco Guicciardini says that it was given him by the customary arrogance of the Spaniards.
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the command in Italy twice. In 1495 he was sent with a small force of little more than five thousand men to aid Ferdinand I of Naples, by then brother in law of king Ferdinand II of Aragon to recover his kingdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT Path to Italian success through earlier failures
2 CONT
2 CONT His first battle in Italy at Seminara in 1495, resulted in defeat at the hands of the French led by Bernard Stewart d'Aubigny. The following year, avoiding pitched battle, he captured the rebel county of Alvito for the king, drivi
2 CONC ng the French back to Calabria.
2 CONT
2 CONT He returned home in 1498. After a brief interval of service against the conquered Moors who had risen in revolt, he was back to Italy in 1501. Ferdinand II of Aragon had entered into his apparently iniquitous compact with Louis XI
2 CONC I of France for the spoliation and division of the kingdom of Naples. The Great Captain was chosen to command the Spanish part of the coalition. As general and as viceroy of Naples he remained in Italy till 1507.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his first command he was mostly employed in Calabria in mountain warfare which bore much resemblance to his former experience in Granada. There was, however, a material difference in the enemy. The French forces under d'Aub
2 CONC igny consisted largely of Swiss mercenary pikemen, and of their own men-at-arms, the heavily armoured professional cavalry, the gendarmes. With his veterans of the Granadine war, foot soldiers armed with sword and buckler, or arqu
2 CONC ebuses and crossbows, and light cavalry, who possessed endurance unparalleled among the soldiers of the time, he could carry on a guerrilla-like warfare which wore down his opponents, who suffered far more than the Spaniards from 
2 CONC the heat.
2 CONT
2 CONT His experience at Seminara showed him that something more was wanted on the battlefield. The action was lost mainly because Ferdinand, disregarding the advice of Gonzalo, persisted in fighting a pitched battle with their more ligh
2 CONC tly equipped troops. In the open field, the loose formation and short swords of the Spanish infantry put them at a disadvantage against a charge of heavy cavalry or pikemen. Gonzalo therefore introduced a closer formation, and div
2 CONC ided the Spanish infantry into the battle or main central body of pikemen, and the wings of shot, called a colunella - the original pike and shot formation.
2 CONT
2 CONT The French were expelled by 1498 without another battle, king Charles VIII of France diyng in April 1498. When the Great Captain reappeared in Italy he had first to perform the congenial task of driving the Turks out of Kefalonia,
2 CONC  together with such condottieri as Pedro Navarro, helping the Venetian navy to reconquer the Castle of Saint Georges, 25 december 1500, killing there over 300 people including Albanian born Gisdar, to aid in the campaign against F
2 CONC rederick IV of Naples.
2 CONT
2 CONT Franco-Spanish battles after deposing Frederick IV of Naples
2 CONT
2 CONT When the king of Naples had been deposed, the French and Spaniards engaged in a guerilla war while they negotiated the partition of the kingdom. The Great Captain now found himself with a much outnumbered army besieged in Barletta
2 CONC  by the French. The war was divided into two phases very similar to one another. During the end of 1502 and the early part of 1503 the Spaniards were besieged in Barletta near the Ofanto on the shores of the Adriatic. Cordoba reso
2 CONC lutely refused to be tempted into battle either by the taunts of the French or the discontent of his own soldiers. Meanwhile he employed the Aragonese partisans in the country, and flying expeditions of his own men, to harass the 
2 CONC enemy's communications and distracted his men with a tournament between Italian knights under Ettore Fieramosca and French prisoners.
2 CONT
2 CONT When he was reinforced, and the French committed the mistake of spreading out their forces to forage for supplies, he took the offensive, and pounced on his enemies supply depot in the Cerignola, there he took up a strong defensiv
2 CONC e position (he was still outnumbered three to one), there he threw up hasty field works, and strengthened them with a species of wire entanglements. The French made a headlong front attack, were repulsed, assailed in flank, and ro
2 CONC uted in only half an hour by the combination of firepower and defensive measures. Later operations on the Garigliano against Ludovico II of Saluzzo were very similar, and led to the total expulsion of the French from the Kingdom o
2 CONC f Naples.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later life
2 CONT
2 CONT Cordoba was appointed Viceroy of Naples in 1504. However, his fame aroused the jealousy of so typical a renaissance monarch as Ferdinand II of Aragon. Furthermore, Cordoba was profligate in using the public treasury to reward his 
2 CONC captains and soldiers. The death of queen Isabel I in 1504 deprived him of a friend and protector and in 1507 he was recalled. Ferdinand loaded him with titles and fine words, but left him unemployed till his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Córdoba was first among the founders of modern warfare. As a field commander, Córdoba, like Napoleon three centuries later, saw his goal in the destruction of the enemy army. He systematically organized the pursuit of defeated arm
2 CONC ies after a victory in order to destroy the retreating enemy. Córdoba helped found the first modern standing army and the nearly invincible Spanish infantry that dominated battlefields of Europe during 16th and first decades of 17
2 CONC th centuries.
2 CONT
2 CONT The best generals of Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor and Philip II of Spain were either the pupils of the Great Captain or were trained by them.
2 CONT
2 CONT Córdoba's influence upon tactics was profound. Wellington's Torres Vedras campaign has a distinct resemblance to Córdoba's campaign at Barletta and the Battle of Assaye is easily compared with that at Garigliano.
2 CONT
2 CONT He left no sons, so he was succeeded in his dukedoms by his daughter, Elvira Fernández de Córdoba y Manrique.
2 CONT
2 CONT His burial place, Monastery of San Jerónimo, Granada, is a magnificent piece of Renaissance Architecture built by his wife and daughter. It was desecrated by French Napoleonic troops under the command of Corsican General Sebastian
2 CONC i at the beginning of the 19th century. Stone from the tower was used to build the "Puente Verde" bridge over the river Genil. The monastery was fully restored at the end of the 19th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Renown
2 CONT
2 CONT Gonzalo's renown in Spain was great and many of the conquistadors admired him, some even imitating his dress fashion. Many had served under him like Amador de Lares who was steward to the Great Captain.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba y Aguilar (Montilla, (Casa de Aguilar), 1 de septiembre de 1453 – Loja (Granada), 2 de diciembre de 1515) fue un noble, político y militar español, duque de Santángelo, Terranova, Andría, Montalto y Se
2 CONC ssa, llamado por su excelencia en la guerra el Gran Capitán.
2 CONT
2 CONT Comandante español al servicio de los Reyes cristianos. Miembro de la nobleza andaluza (perteneciente a la Casa de Aguilar), hijo segundo del noble caballero Pedro Fernández de Aguilar, V Señor de Aguilar de la Frontera y de Prieg
2 CONC o de Córdoba, que murió muy mozo, y de Elvira de Herrera y Enríquez, hija de Pedro Núñez de Herrera, señor de Pedraza y de Blanca Enríquez de Mendoza, quien fue hija de Alonso Enríquez, almirante de Castilla (hijo de Fadrique Alfo
2 CONC nso de Castilla) y de Juana de Mendoza "la Ricahembra".
2 CONT
2 CONT El Ducado de Sessa es un título nobiliario español concedido por los Reyes Católicos en 1507 a Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba, el Gran Capitán. Su nombre se refiere al municipio italiano de Sessa Aurunca, en la provincia de Caserta.
2 CONC   Considerado desde el siglo XVI como título de Castilla, su denominación en italiano es Duca di Sessa.
2 CONT
2 CONT www.wikipedia.com
