1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Wilhelm I von der Mark-Arenberg van Lummen=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Johann I, von der Mark Edelherr von Arenberg, Lord of Sedan & Arenberg and Countess Anna Agnes von Virneburg
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://nl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Willem_I_van_der_Marck Willem I van der Marck, by Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT Willem van der Marck (French: Guillaume de La Marck ) (c. 1446 – Maastricht, June 18, 1485 ), Lord of Lummen (French: Lumey or Lumain ), nicknamed The Boar of the Ardennes ( Le Sanglier des Ardennes ) or William with the Beard , w
2 CONC as one of the most important nobles of the Prince-Bishopric of Liège
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Life Course'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Van der Marck was the third son of Jan van der Marck-Arenberg, Lord of Arenberg , Sedan , and Lummen (- 1470 ), and Anna van Virneburg (- 1480 ). Van der Marck's coat of arms consisted of a wild boar. Due to the recognizable banne
2 CONC r he carried during the wars in Liège, he was thus ambiguously called the boar of the Ardennes .
2 CONT
2 CONT The Van der Marck family originally came from German Westphalia and had settled in the Southern Netherlands , where the family had acquired numerous properties in the Ardennes and the Meuse Valley. Jan's son Robert had inherited t
2 CONC he Lordship of Sedan, Everard the Lordship of Arenberg , and Willem the Lordship of Lummen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Van der Marck's name first appears in 1467 , when he supported the Burgundian Duke Charles the Bold in his war against Liège . In 1474, on another occasion, he burned down the Abbey of Saint-Laurent Momalle and killed the Vicar Ge
2 CONC neral of the Prince-Bishop of Liège, Richard Troncillon . After Charles the Bold was killed at Nancy on January 5, 1477 , Van der Marck defected to the Prince-Bishop and asked for forgiveness. Through the intervention of his broth
2 CONC er Everard, he was not treated as a prisoner of war. In 1477, Van der Marck received the fortress of Franchimont from the Prince-Bishop of Liège, Louis of Bourbon , who attempted to win his friendship in this way. Due to Van der M
2 CONC arck's military reinforcement of the fortress, Louis reversed his decision and banished Van der Marck from Liège. He fled to France and concluded an agreement with the French King Louis XI . Van der Marck promised him free passage
2 CONC  through Liège in case the French wanted to conquer Brabant . The French king offered him one hundred lancers and 30,000 ecu in return.
2 CONT
2 CONT With this French support, Van der Marck assassinated the Prince-Bishop of Liège, Louis of Bourbon, in 1482 and replaced him with his son, Jan van der Marck , which ultimately failed. Another story is that Van der Marck personally 
2 CONC beheaded the bishop with a battle axe. He was unable to have his son confirmed as the new bishop, and consequently, Johan van Horne was elected the new Prince-Bishop of Liège. This led to a civil war in the Prince-Bishopric of Liè
2 CONC ge. On May 21, 1484 , a treaty was signed in Tongeren , in which Van der Marck renounced his claims to the bishopric and Liège. Subsequently, he supported Jan van Horne in the struggle against Maximilian I , the future Emperor of 
2 CONC the Holy Roman Empire , in exchange for a payment of 30,000 livres. Van der Marck received the Castle of Bouillon as collateral for this.
2 CONT
2 CONT Willem van der Marck held out in Liège until mid-1484. He surrendered to the besiegers on the condition that he would be granted forgiveness. Emperor Maximilian is said to have given him this promise, but broke it the following ye
2 CONC ar. [ 1 ] On June 17, 1485, Willem van der Marck was lured into a trap in Sint-Truiden by supporters of the Prince-Bishop of Liège. At a feast, Frederik de Montigny challenged him to a horse race from Sint-Truiden to Halmaal . Wil
2 CONC lem participated and was thus separated from his friends and allies. He was captured in Brabant territory and on June 18, 1485, in Maastricht, he was condemned without trial [ 2 ] and executed by beheading on the scaffold in the V
2 CONC rijthof in Maastricht. [ 3 ]
2 CONT
2 CONT Van der Marck's cousin Everhard van der Marck was Prince-Bishop of Liège from 1506 to 1538. His great-grandson Willem II van der Marck Lumey was a leader of the Sea Beggars in the Eighty Years' War , responsible for, among other t
2 CONC hings, the murder of the Martyrs of Gorinchem and Cornelis Musius in 1572 .
2 CONT
2 CONT Van der Marck is described by Walter Scott as 'William, Count of la Marck' in Quentin Durward (1823). This story inspired the French painter Delacroix to paint Massacre de l'évêque de Liége (Massacre of the Bishop of Liège).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Willem I van der Mark was married to Johanna van Aarschot-Schoonhoven and had the following children:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Johann I, Graf von der Marck-Lummen''' († 1519)
2 CONT * '''Willem van der Mark'''  († 1516), knight and lord of Aigremont, Montbazon, Sainte-Maure etc., chamberlain of King Louis XII
2 CONT * '''Margareta van der Mark''' († 1509), married to Lancelot van Berlaymont and Frederik van Sombreffe
2 CONT * '''Maria Isabella van der Mark, '''married to Jean de Lorraine († 1510)
2 CONT
