1 NOTE Princesa de Asturias (1468-1474); Reina de Castilla, León, Galicia, Toledo, Murcia, Sevilla, Córdoba, Jaén, Algeciras, Gibraltar, Isalas Canarias (1474-1504); Sra. de Vizcaya y Molina; Reina de Granada, de las Isalas y Tierras fir
2 CONC mes del Mar Océano (1492-1504).
1 NOTE Isabella I (1451-1504), queen of Castile, called la Católica (the 
2 CONT Catholic.). She was the daughter of John II of Castile and León by his 
2 CONT second wife, Isabella of Portugal. In 1469 Princess Isabella married 
2 CONT Ferdinand of Aragón, known also as Ferdinand V, The Catholic, and on the 
2 CONT death of her brother, Henry IV, Isabella and Ferdinand jointly succeeded 
2 CONT (1474) to the throne of Castile and León. Isabella's succession was 
2 CONT contested, however, by Alfonso V of Portugal, who supported the claim of 
2 CONT Henry's daughter Juana la Beltraneja (1462-1530). Alfonso attacked Castile 
2 CONT and León but was defeated by the Castilian army in 1476. Three years later 
2 CONT Ferdinand succeeded to the throne of Aragón. This union of the two main 
2 CONT Spanish kingdoms laid the foundation of Spain's future greatness. Isabella 
2 CONT and her husband (known together as “the Catholic kings”) are remembered 
2 CONT for completing the reconquest of Spain from the Moors, for initiating the 
2 CONT Inquisition, and for their ruthless expulsion of the Spanish Jews. They 
2 CONT are perhaps best known, however, for having sponsored the voyages of 
2 CONT Christopher Columbus.
1 NOTE OR "ISABELLA"; QUEEN OF SPAIN 1474-1504
1 NOTE Princesa de Asturias (1468-1474); Reina de Castilla, León, Galicia, Toledo, Murcia, Sevilla, Córdoba, Jaén, Algeciras, Gibraltar, Isalas Canarias (1474-1504); Sra. de Vizcaya y Molina; Reina de Granada, de las Isalas y Tierras fir
2 CONC mes del Mar Océano (1492-1504).
1 NOTE Ruled from 1474 to 1504. Daughter of Juan II de Castile and Leon. and second wife, Isabella of Portugal.
2 CONT In 1469 she married King Fernando de Aragon. She succeeded her brother, Enrique IV.
2 CONT There was trouble brewing though, because Alfonso of Portugal was supporting the claim of the throne of Spain of Juana la Beltraneja, the daughter of Enrique IV. As a result Alfonso of Portugal attacked Castile and Leon but was de
2 CONC feated by the Castillian army in 1476. Three years later her husband became King Ferdinando of Aragon. This matrimonial union between Ferdinando and Isabele was also the union of two main Spanish kingdoms and laid the foundation o
2 CONC f Spain's future.
2 CONT In 1478 Isabela I and her husband initiated the infamous Inquisition. They also finished what their ancestors had begun so many centuries before them, the reconquest of Spain from the Moors.
2 CONT They also were ruthless in their explusion of the Spanish Jews, both events happened in 1492.
2 CONT It was also in 1492 they sponsored Christopher Columbus' voyage to find a trade route to Asia. This later opened up a venue for setting up of an over seas colonial empire for Spain. It brought vast wealth and power to Spain at the
2 CONC  cost of many lives.
