0 @N6000000002188378046@ NOTE Juana Enríquez
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Juana Enríquez
1 CONT Queen consort of Aragon, Majorca, Valencia, and Sicily; Countess consort of Barcelona (more...)
1 CONT Consort 1458–1468
1 CONT Consort to John II
1 CONT Issue
1 CONT Ferdinand II
1 CONT Joanna, Queen of Naples
1 CONT Royal house House of Trastamara
1 CONT House of Enríquez
1 CONT Father Fadrique Enríquez, Count of Melba and Rueda
1 CONT Mother Mariana de Córdoba y Ayala
1 CONT Born 1425
1 CONT Died 13 February 1468
1 CONT
1 CONT Juana Enriquez de Córdoba (1425 - 13 February 1468) was a Castilian noblewoman who became Queen of the Kingdoms of the Crown of Aragon.
1 CONT
1 CONT She was a daughter of Fadrique Enríquez, Count of Melba and Rueda and Mariana de Córdoba. Juana married John I of Navarre in April 1444, three years after death of his first wife, Queen Blanche I of Navarre. Although John ceased t
1 CONC o be de jure uxoris monarch of Navarre on his wife's death, he never ceded power to his heir, Charles, Prince of Viana, and Juana was thus styled Queen of Navarre. Juana supported her husband in his decision not to cede power to h
1 CONC is son and later to his daughter, who was de jure Blanche II of Navarre. Such breaking of the law of succession and the conflict between farmers and nobles led to the outbreak of Navarrese Civil War. Accused of having ordered the 
1 CONC poisoning of Carloher step-son, Juana fled to Girona, seeking the protection of the bishop.
1 CONT
1 CONT Juana became Queen of Aragon, Majorca, Valencia, and Sicily in 1458 on the death of her broher-in-law, King Alfonso V of Aragon.
1 CONT
1 CONT Queen Juana's children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Fernando II of Aragon who married the future Queen Isabella I of Castile and reigned with her as King of Castile
1 CONT     * Joanna who married Ferdinand I of Naples and thus became Queen of Naples.
1 CONT
1 CONT Juana's greatest wish was to have her son married to Infanta Isabella, half-sister and heiress presumptive of King Henry IV of Castile. Ferdinand did marry Isabella and their marriage, which lasted for 35 years, produced a prince 
1 CONC and four queens. However, Juana died on 13 February 1468 from breast cancer, a year before the marriage occured. She was survived by her husband, who never remarried and reigned until his death in 1479.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT Spanish royalty
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Maria of Castile Queen consort of Aragon, Majorca, Valencia and Sicily
1 CONT Countess consort of Barcelona
1 CONT 1458–1468 Succeeded by
1 CONT Isabella I of Castille
